{"version": 2, "width": 100, "height": 30, "timestamp": 1787286245, "idle_time_limit": 5.0, "env": {"SHELL": "/bin/bash", "TERM": "xterm-256color"}}
[0.041076, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1m  Mental Health Knowledge Graph\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  Which help exists — for whom, in what language, at what price,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  and is there anyone actually there to provide it?\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n"]
[1.177064, "o", "  \u001b[1m113,710\u001b[0m nodes   \u001b[1m1,665,153\u001b[0m edges   \u001b[1m4\u001b[0m sources\r\n"]
[5.177222, "o", "\r\n\u001b[2m  [provenance]\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[33m  1. Where did this data come from, and how old is it?\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[5.177639, "o", "\u001b[2m  Before any answer is worth trusting. The graph records its own sources,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  when each was fetched and how often it moves — so it answers this itself\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  rather than asking you to take a caption on trust.\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n  \u001b[36mMATCH (d:DataSource)\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mRETURN d.name AS source, d.publisher AS publisher,\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36md.fetched_on AS fetched, d.real_data AS real_data\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n"]
[7.180991, "o", "  \u001b[1msource             publisher                 fetched     real_data\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[2m-----------------  ------------------------  ----------  ---------\u001b[0m\r\n  Synthea            The MITRE Corporation     2026-08-12  false    \r\n  NPPES              CMS / National Plan and…  2026-08-13  true     \r\n  HRSA               Health Resources and Se…  2026-08-13  true     \r\n  FindTreatment.gov  SAMHSA / BHSIS            2026-08-10  true     \r\n\r\n  \u001b[32m3 ms\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[11.181183, "o", "\r\n\u001b[2m  [level 1 — one label]\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[33m  2. How much help exists, and in what languages?\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  Every US behavioural-health facility, and the languages each one speaks.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  A spreadsheet answers this. It is the floor, not the argument.\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n  \u001b[36mMATCH (f:Facility)-[:SPEAKS]->(l:Language)\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mRETURN l.name AS language, count(f) AS facilities\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mORDER BY facilities DESC LIMIT 5\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n"]
[13.185915, "o", "  \u001b[1mlanguage                                              facilities\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[2m----------------------------------------------------  ----------\u001b[0m\r\n  Sign language services for the deaf and hard of hea…  6,023     \r\n"]
[13.186208, "o", "  Spanish                                               4,558     \r\n  Other languages (excluding Spanish)                   1,276     \r\n  French                                                345       \r\n  Russian                                               329       \r\n\r\n  \u001b[32m4 ms\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[17.186497, "o", "\r\n\u001b[2m  [level 2 — a join]\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[33m  3. Which states serve survivors of intimate partner violence?\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  6,072 facilities nationally are tagged as serving survivors of partner\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  violence — CanopyCare's exact user population, in a free federal dataset.\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n  \u001b[36mMATCH (f:Facility)-[:OFFERS]->(s:Service)\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mWHERE s.value = \"Clients who have experienced intimate partner violence, domestic violence\"\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mRETURN f.state AS state, count(f) AS ipv_facilities\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[17.186584, "o", "  \u001b[36mORDER BY ipv_facilities DESC LIMIT 6\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n"]
[19.198957, "o", "  \u001b[1mstate  ipv_facilities\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[2m-----  --------------\u001b[0m\r\n  CA     563           \r\n  NY     427           \r\n"]
[19.199081, "o", "  FL     296           \r\n  OH     286           \r\n  MD     242           \r\n  PA     227           \r\n\r\n  \u001b[32m12 ms\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[23.19938, "o", "\r\n\u001b[2m  [level 3 — three conditions, one facility]\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[33m  4. She needs Spanish, and cannot pay the full fee. Who can take her?\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  Survivor population, sliding fee scale and Spanish, all true of the same\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  facility, in her state. In a normalised schema this is three self-joins\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  against 1.4 million service rows. Here it is one pattern.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[23.19983, "o", "\r\n  \u001b[36mMATCH (f:Facility)-[:OFFERS]->(a:Service), (f)-[:OFFERS]->(b:Service),\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36m(f)-[:SPEAKS]->(l:Language)\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mWHERE a.value = \"Clients who have experienced intimate partner violence, domestic violence\"\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mAND b.value = \"Sliding fee scale (fee is based on income and other factors)\"\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mAND l.name = \"Spanish\" AND f.state = \"MA\"\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mRETURN count(DISTINCT f.facility_id) AS matching_facilities\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n"]
[25.218973, "o", "  \u001b[1mmatching_facilities\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[2m-------------------\u001b[0m\r\n  26                 \r\n\r\n  \u001b[32m19 ms\u001b[0m"]
[25.218995, "o", "\r\n"]
[29.219325, "o", "\r\n\u001b[2m  [level 3 — the actual answer]\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[33m  5. Name them, with a number to call.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  This is the difference the graph makes to a survivor: named places with\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  intake numbers, instead of one generic national hotline.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[29.219752, "o", "\r\n  \u001b[36mMATCH (f:Facility)-[:OFFERS]->(a:Service), (f)-[:OFFERS]->(b:Service),\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36m(f)-[:SPEAKS]->(l:Language)\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mWHERE a.value = \"Clients who have experienced intimate partner violence, domestic violence\"\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mAND b.value = \"Sliding fee scale (fee is based on income and other factors)\"\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mAND l.name = \"Spanish\" AND f.state = \"MA\"\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[29.219995, "o", "  \u001b[36mRETURN DISTINCT f.name AS facility, f.city AS city,\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mcoalesce(f.intake, f.phone) AS call\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mLIMIT 5\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n"]
[31.232738, "o", "  \u001b[1mfacility                            city          call           \u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[2m----------------------------------  ------------  ---------------\u001b[0m\r\n  Advocates Community Counseling      Marlborough   508-661-2038   \r\n  Community Counseling of Bristol C…  Taunton       508-828-9116   \r\n  Uplift Behavioral Health Services   West Roxbury  617-942-6040   \r\n"]
[31.232898, "o", "  Bay Cove Treatment Center           Boston        617-371-3030 x1\r\n  High Point Multi Service Center     Plymouth      774-773-6668   \r\n\r\n  \u001b[32m13 ms\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[35.232933, "o", "\r\n\u001b[2m  [level 4 — absence]\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[33m  6. Trauma counselling — but NOT opioid-only programmes.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  138 facilities match before the exclusion; 127 after. The eleven removed\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  are opioid-use-disorder-only programmes that would turn her away.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  An embedding cannot represent a service a facility does not offer.\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n  \u001b[36mMATCH (f:Facility)-[:OFFERS]->(a:Service), (f)-[:OFFERS]->(t:Service)\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mWHERE a.value = \"Clients who have experienced intimate partner violence, domestic violence\"\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mAND t.value = \"Trauma-related counseling\" AND f.state = \"MA\"\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mWITH f OPTIONAL MATCH (f)-[:OFFERS]->(x:Service)\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mWHERE x.value = \"Opioid use disorder clients only\"\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mWITH f, x\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mRETURN count(DISTINCT f.facility_id) AS before_exclusion,\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mcount(DISTINCT CASE WHEN x IS NULL THEN f.facility_id END) AS after_exclusion\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n"]
[37.248903, "o", "  \u001b[1mbefore_exclusion  after_exclusion\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[2m----------------  ---------------\u001b[0m\r\n  138               127            \r\n"]
[37.248927, "o", "\r\n  \u001b[32m16 ms\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[41.249258, "o", "\r\n\u001b[2m  [level 4 — a gap]\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[33m  7. Which languages is Vermont missing entirely?\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  Not 'which are rare' — which are absent from the whole state. Vermont has\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  27 facilities serving survivors and not one of them speaks Spanish.\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n  \u001b[36mMATCH (l:Language)\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[41.249356, "o", "  \u001b[36mWITH l OPTIONAL MATCH (f:Facility)-[:SPEAKS]->(l)\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mWHERE f.state = \"VT\"\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mWITH l, count(f) AS vt_facilities WHERE vt_facilities = 0\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mRETURN l.name AS absent_from_vermont\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mORDER BY l.name DESC LIMIT 6\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n"]
[43.265428, "o", "  \u001b[1mabsent_from_vermont\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[2m-------------------\u001b[0m\r\n  Yupik              \r\n  Vietnamese         \r\n  Tagalog            \r\n"]
[43.265515, "o", "  Spanish            \r\n  Portuguese         \r\n  Polish             \r\n\r\n  \u001b[32m16 ms\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[47.265625, "o", "\r\n\u001b[2m  [level 5 — the demand side]\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[33m  8. Who needs that help, and where do they live?\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  Supply is only half the question. Synthea (MITRE, Apache 2.0) generates a\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  synthetic population with conditions and geography — no real person is in\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  this graph. The partner-abuse finding is lifetime screening prevalence,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  not current need.\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n  \u001b[36mMATCH (p:Patient)-[:HAS_CONDITION]->(c:Condition)\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mWHERE c.name = \"Victim of intimate partner abuse (finding)\"\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mRETURN p.state AS state, count(DISTINCT p.patient_id) AS survivors\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mORDER BY survivors DESC\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n"]
[49.271854, "o", "  \u001b[1mstate  survivors\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[2m-----  ---------\u001b[0m\r\n  MA     790      \r\n  VT     617      \r\n\r\n  \u001b[32m6 ms\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[53.27262, "o", "\r\n\u001b[2m  [level 5 — four sources at once]\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[33m  9. How many of them live somewhere the government already calls under-served?\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  Synthea says who and where. HRSA says which counties are designated mental-\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  health shortage areas. Neither publisher joins to the other, and nobody\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  publishes the intersection: 1,314 of 1,407 survivors live in a designated\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  county. Here is where they are, worst-served first.\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n  \u001b[36mMATCH (p:Patient)-[:HAS_CONDITION]->(c:Condition),\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36m(p)-[:IN_COUNTY]->(ct:County)-[:IN_STATE]->(s:State),\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36m(sa:ShortageArea)-[:COVERS]->(ct)\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mWHERE c.name = \"Victim of intimate partner abuse (finding)\"\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mRETURN s.code AS state, ct.name AS county,\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mround(avg(sa.score)) AS shortage_score,\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mcount(DISTINCT p.patient_id) AS survivors\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mORDER BY survivors DESC LIMIT 6\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n"]
[55.327684, "o", "  \u001b[1mstate  county      shortage_score  survivors\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[2m-----  ----------  --------------  ---------\u001b[0m\r\n  MA     Middlesex   19              179      \r\n  VT     Chittenden  21              152      \r\n  MA     Norfolk     16              92       \r\n  MA     Suffolk     17              86       \r\n  MA     Essex       21              81       \r\n  MA     Bristol     14              79       \r\n\r\n  \u001b[32m55 ms\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[59.328029, "o", "\r\n\u001b[2m  [level 5 — is anyone there?]\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[59.328343, "o", "\u001b[1m\u001b[33m  10. A facility existing is not a clinician existing. Is anyone there?\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  The last layer: 82,978 clinicians from the federal provider register,\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  counted per state and speciality. Vermont's survivor-serving facilities\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  sit against a fraction of Massachusetts' psychiatric workforce.\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n"]
[59.328614, "o", "  \u001b[36mMATCH (f:Facility)-[:OFFERS]->(a:Service),\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36m(f)-[:LOCATED_IN]->(s:State)-[h:HAS_PROVIDERS]->(t:Taxonomy)\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mWHERE a.value = \"Clients who have experienced intimate partner violence, domestic violence\"\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mAND t.code = \"2084P0800X\" AND s.code IN [\"MA\", \"VT\"]\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mRETURN s.code AS state, count(DISTINCT f.facility_id) AS ipv_facilities,\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mh.count AS psychiatrists\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[36mORDER BY ipv_facilities DESC\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n"]
[61.392338, "o", "  \u001b[1mstate  ipv_facilities  psychiatrists\u001b[0m\r\n  \u001b[2m-----  --------------  -------------\u001b[0m\r\n  MA     159             3,150        \r\n  VT     27              241          \r\n\r\n  \u001b[32m64 ms\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[65.392449, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1m\u001b[32m  One graph, four federal and simulated sources, one query language.\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  Supply from FindTreatment.gov · shortage designations from HRSA ·\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  clinical capacity from NPPES · demand simulated with Synthea.\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n\u001b[2m  Public federal records only. No survivor, session or transcript is\u001b[0m\r\n\u001b[2m  stored anywhere in this graph.\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n"]
